    <foaf:age rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">26</foaf:age>
    <villain:distinctiveFeatures>Hat</villain:distinctiveFeatures>
    <villain:personalityTraits>Brazenness; Chrisma; Psychopath; Music; Sympathetic Villain; Dual Personality</villain:personalityTraits>
    <villain:hasPersonalityTrait rdf:resource="http://villainscope.org/trait/Brazenness"/>
    <villain:hasPersonalityTrait rdf:resource="http://villainscope.org/trait/Chrisma"/>
    <villain:hasPersonalityTrait rdf:resource="http://villainscope.org/trait/Psychopath"/>
    <villain:hasPersonalityTrait rdf:resource="http://villainscope.org/trait/Music"/>
    <villain:hasPersonalityTrait rdf:resource="http://villainscope.org/trait/Sympathetic_Villain"/>
    <villain:hasPersonalityTrait rdf:resource="http://villainscope.org/trait/Dual_Personality"/>
    <villain:personalityTrait>Brazenness</villain:personalityTrait>
    <villain:personalityTrait>Chrisma</villain:personalityTrait>
    <villain:personalityTrait>Psychopath</villain:personalityTrait>
    <villain:personalityTrait>Music</villain:personalityTrait>
    <villain:personalityTrait>Sympathetic Villain</villain:personalityTrait>
    <villain:personalityTrait>Dual Personality</villain:personalityTrait>
    <villain:personalityDetails>Music: Leitmotif. Symbolic Tune - Beckert incessantly whistles Edvard Grieg's "In the Hall of the Mountain King". This specific piece is symbolic, as it originally accompanied a scene where trolls sing about slaughtering the protagonist. The Trigger - The whistling functions as a nervous "tic" and a psychological "trigger" that awakens Beckert’s uncontrollable side shortly before he commits an atrocity. Evidence of Lack of Control -This musical leitmotif serves as a key piece of evidence that his personality is "evenly bisected" and that he cannot govern his own behavior, which ultimately leads the audience to take pity on him. | Sympathetic Villain: He is depicted as a man suffering from intense pain and anguish because he kills against his will. By portraying him as a pathetic victim of his own impulses, Lang forces the audience to feel empathy for a man who would normally be considered a monster. | Dual Personality: Reveal the "torments of his soul" and the internal struggle between his stable self and the "demons" that drive him to kill.</villain:personalityDetails>
    <schema1:description>Music: Leitmotif. Symbolic Tune - Beckert incessantly whistles Edvard Grieg's "In the Hall of the Mountain King". This specific piece is symbolic, as it originally accompanied a scene where trolls sing about slaughtering the protagonist. The Trigger - The whistling functions as a nervous "tic" and a psychological "trigger" that awakens Beckert’s uncontrollable side shortly before he commits an atrocity. Evidence of Lack of Control -This musical leitmotif serves as a key piece of evidence that his personality is "evenly bisected" and that he cannot govern his own behavior, which ultimately leads the audience to take pity on him. | Sympathetic Villain: He is depicted as a man suffering from intense pain and anguish because he kills against his will. By portraying him as a pathetic victim of his own impulses, Lang forces the audience to feel empathy for a man who would normally be considered a monster. | Dual Personality: Reveal the "torments of his soul" and the internal struggle between his stable self and the "demons" that drive him to kill.</schema1:description>
  </rdf:Description>
